 ## What you still have to do
 
-Chronicle erases keys. It does not know what else your system did with the data.
+Treat a successful key deletion as evidence for one part of your erasure procedure. Before recording the request as complete:
+
+1. Repeat the call in every relevant namespace and resolve any incomplete erasures. Check failed observer partitions for that event source: a forwarding subscription or reactor still producing PII must stop, unless you deliberately authorize a new key.
+2. Rebuild stale stored read models using a clean destination or an audited migration. Projection and reducer replay materialize erased PII as empty markers without creating a key or removing the fence; live subjects retain normal protection. You do not need a new key just to rebuild. Replaying over existing storage does not prove cleanup; follow the [read-model rebuild procedure](read-models.mdx#rebuilding-after-erasure).
+3. Handle copies outside Chronicle, including warehouse exports, search indexes and data sent to third parties. Also assess historical causation values separately from encrypted event content.
+4. Define what happens when a backup restores the key and removes its fence. Restore the key store consistently with the rest of storage and block access until required erasures have been reapplied. See [what the fence cannot protect against](key-lifecycle.mdx#not-stopped) and [backup and restore ordering](../hosting/configuration/compliance-storage.md#backup-and-restore-ordering).
 
-- **Erase in every namespace the person appears in.** One call per namespace; there is no cross-namespace erasure, by design.
-- **Deal with the failed partition, if there is one.** An event carrying `[PII]` for a subject you erased cannot be appended, so a forwarding subscription or a reactor that keeps producing them will report a failed partition for that event source. That is the signal that something is still writing the person's data — either stop it, or authorize a new key.
-- **Rebuild stored read models without restarting protection.** Projection and reducer replay materialize an erased subject's PII as empty markers, without creating a key or removing the erasure fence. Live subjects remain protected normally. You do not need to authorize a new key just to rebuild. For stale or wrong-subject copies, use a clean destination or an audited migration; replaying over existing storage is not a scrub — see [read-model rebuilds](read-models.mdx#rebuilding-after-erasure).
-- **Record the *who*.** Chronicle logs that an erasure completed, which event stores it reached, and the subject as a one-way binding rather than by name — see [what Chronicle records](key-lifecycle). What it cannot know, and therefore cannot record, is who asked for the erasure and under what legal basis. That part is yours.
-- **Chase the copies outside Chronicle.** Read models exported to a warehouse, search indexes, and anything a reactor sent to a third party are outside the key store and outside the erasure.
-- **Keep erasures from coming back with a restore.** A backup of the key store taken before the erasure still holds the key, and restoring it brings the key back without any error. Plan a restore-and-re-erase step, and restore the key store at the same point in time as the rest of the storage — see [what the fence cannot protect against](key-lifecycle.mdx#not-stopped) and [backup and restore ordering](../hosting/configuration/compliance-storage.md#backup-and-restore-ordering).
+Record who requested and authorized the erasure, and under what legal basis. Chronicle logs completion, the event stores reached and a one-way subject binding instead of the person's name, but it does not know the actor. [What Chronicle records](key-lifecycle) explains how to use those logs alongside your application's record.
 
