-title: Understanding constraints
-description: How Chronicle enforces uniqueness without a unique index, with [Unique] or an IConstraint class checked in the kernel before anything commits.
+title: Cratis Chronicle constraints for uniqueness and stream closure
+description: Cratis Chronicle checks declared constraints before appending events. Learn how to prevent duplicate values and choose guards for state-based rules.
 ---
 
-One question stops a lot of teams at the door of event sourcing, and it's a good one: **"If state is derived from events, how do I stop two users registering the same email?"** There's no table to put a `UNIQUE` index on.
+Cratis Chronicle constraints check declared rules in the server kernel before an event is appended. Use a uniqueness constraint when two event sources must not claim the same value, such as a registration email. The check belongs with the append, not in a read model that may be behind the event log.
 
-Chronicle's answer is **constraints** — rules you declare next to your events, enforced in the **kernel** before an event is committed. This page is the mental model: why the check has to live server-side, the two ways to declare a rule, and how a violation surfaces. The exhaustive options live in the [Constraints reference](/chronicle/constraints/).
+This page explains how to choose and declare that rule, handle a rejection, and distinguish uniqueness from a decision that depends on current state. The [Constraints reference](/chronicle/constraints/) covers the options and operational limits.
 
 ## Why the check belongs in the kernel
 
-The worry is real: in a classic CRUD app you lean on a database `UNIQUE` constraint, but here the "current state" is a projection, built *after* the fact. By the time a read model notices a duplicate email, the duplicate event is already in the log. Checking the read model *before* appending doesn't save you either — two requests can both pass the check and both append.
+In a CRUD application, a database `UNIQUE` constraint can reject a duplicate during the write. A materialized projection cannot do that job: it processes events after they are stored. Checking the projection before appending also leaves a race, because two requests can both see the email as available.
 
-Chronicle closes that gap by checking the rule **before the event is committed**, inside the kernel:
+A Chronicle constraint moves the check into the append path:
 
 ```mermaid
 flowchart LR
@@ -20,72 +20,70 @@ flowchart LR
     C -->|already used| R["Append rejected"]
 ```
 
-An append that would introduce a duplicate is *rejected at the source* — it never reaches the log. Because the check lives in the kernel, it's consistent across every client and every entry point: a .NET client, a REST call, or a future integration you haven't written yet. No constraint logic runs in your application code.
+When the kernel finds a conflicting claim, it rejects the append without storing the event. The registered rule applies to writers in its configured scope regardless of which client sent the append. Your application declares the rule and handles the result; it does not run a separate uniqueness query.
+
+A constraint is checked within one namespace and event sequence, not across them. The event is made durable before the constraint index is updated, so those writes are not crash-atomic. Account for index recovery when operating the store; a pre-append check is not a promise that the event and its index share one storage transaction.
 
 ## Declare it on the event
 
-The simplest way to declare a rule is right where the value lives — adorn the event property with `[Unique]`:
+In .NET, a property-level `[Unique]` attribute declares the rule beside the value it protects. Declaration support differs by client; the model-bound reference below identifies the available forms.
 
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="understanding-constraints/property-attribute" />
 
-That one attribute is the whole client-side story. When your application starts, Chronicle scans for `[Unique]`-adorned types, registers the constraint with the kernel, and the kernel builds the indexes it needs to enforce it on every subsequent append.
-
-A few things make this practical:
+The .NET client's convention-based discovery collects the attributes and registers the constraint with the kernel at startup. Give the same `name` to `[Unique]` on `UserRegistered.Email` and `UserEmailChanged.NewEmail` to check registrations and email changes against the same claims. By default, a property constraint retains one value per event source: claiming a new value releases that source's previous value. Choose a retention mode deliberately if earlier values must stay claimed.
 
-- **One rule can span several events.** Give the same `name` to `[Unique]` on `UserRegistered.Email` and `UserEmailChanged.NewEmail`, and a changed email is checked against registered ones too — the rule follows the *value*, not a single event type.
-- **Some events are one-of-a-kind.** Put `[Unique]` on the event *type* instead of a property, and only one event of that type can ever be appended per event source — registering the same user twice becomes impossible by declaration:
+Put `[Unique]` on the event type instead when the rule is one occurrence per event source, such as preventing a second `UserRegistered`:
 
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="understanding-constraints/event-type-attribute" />
 
-- **Values can be released.** When the thing that held the value goes away, mark that event with `[RemoveConstraint]` so the value can be claimed again. A lifecycle that ends in more than one way marks each of those events — every one of them releases the value:
+To release a claim when its owner is removed, declare the removal event with `[RemoveConstraint]`. If several events can end that lifecycle, mark each applicable event so each path releases the claim:
 
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="understanding-constraints/remove-constraint" />
 
-The reference calls this attribute style [model-bound constraints](/chronicle/constraints/model-bound/) — every parameter and combination is documented there.
+The [model-bound constraints reference](/chronicle/constraints/model-bound/) covers names, retention modes, removal, and client declaration support.
 
 ## When the rule outgrows an attribute
 
-Attributes shine when the rule belongs to one event type. Some rules don't: the same logical value appears under *different property names* in different events, the comparison should ignore casing, or the violation message needs to be composed from context. For those, declare the rule in a dedicated class — implement `IConstraint` and describe it with the builder:
+Use a dedicated definition when the rule is easier to read in one place, particularly when several event types contribute values or the violation message needs a callback. In .NET, implement `IConstraint` and describe the rule with its builder:
 
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="understanding-constraints/dedicated-class" />
 
-Read it top to bottom and it says exactly what the rule is: one named constraint, fed by two events with differently named properties, case-insensitive, released when the user is removed, with a friendly message when it fires. The one-of-a-kind variant has a builder form too — `builder.Unique<UserRegistered>()` enforces a single `UserRegistered` per event source.
+The .NET example brings two differently named properties under one constraint, ignores casing, and releases the claim when the user is removed. Its message callback lets the application supply a useful response. The event-type variant has a builder form too: `builder.Unique<UserRegistered>()` declares one `UserRegistered` per event source.
 
